Much of the PEPS package are BATCH script files that in some cases, are 
rather sophisticated [or as some might call them, chock full of kludges 
know to work].  I am neutral on that rating, but there is a lot of things 
you have to know to get certain things to work and often what you want 
seems impossible [yet it is possible, but perhaps obfuscated].  Anyone who 
wants to study this arcane subject will benefit from studying these dozens 
of packages just to know how you get Windows to do what you want [as 
opposed to what it wants, which is often quite different].  And of course, 
I try to document as well as I can, because no one can keep track of all 
the members of bags of tricks [or even which bag the trick came from].

Some of the scripts create transient screen messages that want to 
self-timeout because I will not make it routine to have to ask silly 
questions when time-out  just makes the issue disappear.

I might like some feedback on just this issue.  You could be a beginner an 
really need to stare at an unfamiliar question.  Yet, when you have seen it 
the umpteenth time, you want it gone much more quickly;  Perhaps in a 
future release I can revisit this, but the general rule is this:

1) If you really ought to READ the message [or some situational part of the 
message] there will tend to be a timeout somewhere more than 5 seconds and 
likely less than 10 seconds.  On one semi-complex specific situation, I 
settled on 8 seconds because you probably must read part, even if you 
determine you don't want to see it much more, thus, there are compromises.

2) There are other messages whose purpose is to just keep the user "in the 
loop" that something is happening, but it's gonna take some time.  If they 
happen in rapid succession before a big wait, the timeout may be more like 
3 seconds.

3) If it's your fault that something unexpectedly screws up, you must 
respond to a BATCH pause statement to dismiss and move on.  These are very 
rare and are only on some form of error.

Specific implementation is subject to revision and feedback the takeaway 
for this moment is this is hopeless but not serious, and I appreciate 
anyone moved to respond.  Some will simply not give a damn.  But I MUST 
because I am writing all of this code, etc.

No on to the problem.

There are several ways to implement this conceptually.  Unfortunately, many 
of them are implementation-specific, as in may not work on one of the 
supported configurations, which at this point is for 5 different Windows 
versions, and all could be either 32-bit or 64-bit.

Thus, many nice ideas that were added later are useless when you also have 
to support systems lacking the frill.

There are several homemade "sleep" programs, but some have some copyright 
issues or comppatibility issues and/or it is difficult to use them from a 
seemingly central directory to avoid redundant copies, only to find out 
there is some other gatcha in effect, etc.

As such, I have been working with some IP-based timeouts that apparently 
work in all systems, but are not necessarily documents as being so.

I thought I had this solved, and it was done by using the obscure IP 
address 1.1.1.1 and PINGing it.

Turns out some of the systems are too "smart" and know to NOT wait for the 
stated timeout even thoughi it is stated correctly.

I am trying to change to a more obscure one, using the loopback address of 
127.0.0.1 which enjoys the ability to be used with incompatible syntax not 
generally documented, yet apparently will work.  [Certain systems can 
provide help on the subject, and it only had another mode that won;t work 
with this stated, but the unstated one actually does work.]

Thus, hopefully I have gotten past Obfuscation 'R Us and will hopefully 
have a fully coherent replacement that passes multiple muster.  At least I 
now have the files organized to use less occurrences of that which needs 
thus, so if I have make yet another change, it will be less annoying if 
that is even needed [which would be very annoying.

As such, I am testing as I go, also dealing with some registry-related 
issues that affect Windows 7 and up only [but not necessarily share the 
same exact fix!].

There is an end in site, but it is an unexpected minor setback that has a 
finite lifetime of time-wasting, etc.

If anyone is interested, I can post a generic example of what is being used 
now; it may be of use in your own work [or someone you know, besides me]..

cjl [your not-so-tireless leader]

ps:  I am doing the unthinkable:  My wife and I are taking off an entire 
week in a few days!


